{
  "corpus": "quo",
  "encoding": "hex",
  "area": "arithmetic",
  "note": "The primitives the seal rests on, each with fixed inputs and outputs. A record carrying `source` names the publication its values are taken from, and a record carrying `derived` names the published values it is built on and says how. SHA-256 takes `input` to `hash`. An Ed25519 or X25519 `secret` is the thirty-two bytes a key is made from, as RFC 8032 and RFC 7748 take them, and `pk` is the public key it gives. A signature record carries `pk`, `message` and `signature`, and with `secret` the signature is also the one that secret makes. An X25519 agreement is `secret` with the other side's `pk`, giving `shared`. HKDF-SHA-256 takes `ikm`, `salt` and `info` to `out`, and the salt is always the zero-length salt. The record under `quo-seal` gives forty-four bytes out, `key` the first thirty-two and `nonce` the last twelve, as the sealed box uses them. AES-256-GCM under `key` and `nonce`, `additional` the authenticated data, takes `plaintext` to `ciphertext`, the sixteen-byte tag at its end. A record carrying `shared` in place of `key` and `nonce` is AES-256-GCM under what `quo-seal` draws from that agreement. ML-KEM-768 is in the deterministic internal forms of FIPS 203: `ek` is the encapsulation key of KeyGen_internal(d, z), and `ciphertext` and `shared` are Encaps_internal(ek, m). A record carrying `refuses` is one that must not verify or open.",
